.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-grid\-file=grdfile \-Igrdfile
Sets a GMT grid file to be read and displayed at startup.
.TP
.B \-\-help \-H
Prints a description of the program and its options, then exits.
.TP
.B \-\-overlay\-file=grdfile \-Jgrdfile
Sets a GMT grid file to be read and displayed as an overlay at startup.
This option has no effect unless \fB\-\-grid\-file\fP also sets a primary grid.
.TP
.B \-\-test \-T
Calculates and displays a test grid at startup, so that the program can be
tried without a grid file.
.TP
.B \-\-verbose \-V
Prints the program version and more information about its work to the
stderr stream. Give it twice for debugging output.

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MB_RENDERER
Chooses how the maps and profiles are drawn, once when the program starts.
\fBauto\fP draws with QRhi and the platform's graphics API (Metal on macOS,
Direct3D 11 on Windows, OpenGL on Linux); \fBmetal\fP, \fBd3d11\fP,
\fBd3d12\fP, \fBvulkan\fP and \fBopengl\fP draw with QRhi and that API
where the platform has it, else with the platform's API; \fBlegacy-gl\fP
draws with the legacy OpenGL drawing; \fBnull\fP draws nothing (for tests).
\fBopengl\fP is QRhi's OpenGL, not the legacy OpenGL drawing. On macOS QRhi
cannot draw with OpenGL, whose context there lacks instancing: the views show
the message described below, so there \fBopengl\fP is only for tests.
.IP
Without \fBMB_RENDERER\fP, or with a value not listed above, the program draws
with QRhi on macOS and with the legacy OpenGL drawing on Linux and Windows; a
program built with only one of the two draws with that one. A value it cannot
follow gives one line on the stderr stream saying what it draws with instead.
.IP
The legacy OpenGL drawing is built in two cases: with
\fB\-DbuildQt6LegacyGL=ON\fP (the default except on macOS), and when QRhi is
not built. QRhi needs \fB\-DbuildQt6Rhi=ON\fP (the default), Qt 6.8 or later,
Qt Shader Tools and Qt's private Gui headers (on Debian and Ubuntu the package
qt6-base-private-dev). Where QRhi cannot draw (for example in a virtual machine
without Metal), each view shows a message instead of the
drawing and one line goes to the stderr stream; the program does not change
to the legacy OpenGL drawing by itself.

.SH OTHER PROGRAMS
The Action menu items "Open Selected Nav in MBedit", "Open Selected Nav in
MBeditviz" and "Open Selected Nav in MBvelocitytool" start the Qt6 versions
\fBmbedit-qt\fP, \fBmbeditviz-qt\fP and \fBmbvelocitytool-qt\fP with the
swath files of the selected navigation. "Open Selected Nav in MBnavedit"
starts \fBmbnavedit\fP. A program is looked for in the directory
of \fBmbgrdviz-qt\fP, then on the PATH; if a Qt6 version is not found, the
Motif program is started instead.

.SH DIFFERENCES FROM MBGRDVIZ
The realtime display is not implemented in \fBmbgrdviz\fP either: its Start,
Stop, Pause, Resume and Test controls do nothing there. \fBmbgrdviz-qt\fP
disables them. The Realtime Setup window records the navigation source.

The Mouse menu works as the mouse mode buttons: choosing a site, route or
navigation mode switches on that overlay and redraws the map at once. The
Mouse menu of \fBmbgrdviz\fP switches the overlay on without redrawing.

Pressing Full when the map is already drawn at full resolution shows
"Already at full resolution" in the status line.

Messages name the program \fBmbgrdviz-qt\fP; the files it writes say "Output
by Program MBgrdviz", as those of \fBmbgrdviz\fP do.

Where the maps are drawn with QRhi (see ENVIRONMENT), the grid is drawn with
the detail the zoom calls for. It is drawn coarser only in three cases: for a
few frames while a large part of it is sent to the graphics card (for example
when the grid is opened or recoloured), when the maps would need more than the
graphics memory set aside for them, and while a mouse button is held and the
full detail would draw too slowly. The Resolution dialog of the Controls
menu stays as in \fBmbgrdviz\fP but does not set that detail: its two
dimensions set only the contours shown until the full resolution contours are
made, shortly after the map stops changing, and its two decimations the
navigation in those frames and in the frames that fall back to a lower
resolution while a mouse button is held. A change in it recolours and redraws
the whole map, as in \fBmbgrdviz\fP.

